Package: remlax
Type: Package
Title: Generic, Differentiable REML Solver for Linear Mixed Models
Version: 0.2.0
Authors@R: person(given = "Nicolas", family = "Salas",
                  email = "nicolas.salas@supagro.fr",
                  role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Fits linear mixed models by restricted maximum likelihood
    (REML; Patterson and Thompson (1971) <doi:10.1093/biomet/58.3.545>) with a
    formula grammar close to that of 'ASReml-R'. The R side builds and checks
    the design, writes it to a temporary directory as raw binary files with a
    'JSON' manifest, calls the 'Python' solver as a subprocess and reads the
    result back. The solver evaluates the likelihood either on the dense
    marginal covariance matrix, differentiated automatically with 'JAX' on CPU
    or GPU, or on Henderson's mixed model equations, with a dense or a sparse
    selected inversion. It maximises the likelihood by L-BFGS-B or by average
    information REML (Gilmour, Thompson and Cullis (1995)
    <doi:10.2307/2533274>). Covariance structures follow the 'ASReml-R'
    catalogue (idv, idh, corgh, fa, rr, chol, ante, corh; ar1, ar2, ar3, ma,
    arma, corb, corg, exp, gau, sph, cir, Matern, separable products). Helpers
    build weighted neighbourhood incidences for direct and indirect genetic
    effects in mixtures of two species, as in the model of Salas et al.
    (2026) <doi:10.64898/2026.05.27.728303>. The 'Python' solver ships with
    the package; a 'Python' (>= 3.10)
    interpreter with 'jax', 'numpy' and 'scipy' must be available, and
    rx_install_python() creates one on request. The validation against
    'asreml', 'lme4', 'sommer' and 'nlme' is shipped as a vignette, and
    rx_validate() reruns the comparisons against the packages available on
    CRAN.
License: GPL (>= 3)
